<section class="section section--tight" aria-labelledby="flow-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="flow-title">From patient visit to the books</h2>
    </header>
    <ol class="cycle">
      <li><h3>Bill every visit</h3><p>One numbered bill for each consultation, test or procedure, in one system.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Close the front desk daily</h3><p>Bills against cash counted, UPI and card. A different person should check it where possible.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Separate income types</h3><p>Consultation, procedures, pharmacy, diagnostics and any lab share should sit under different heads.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Record outflows</h3><p>Supplies, rent, salaries, visiting doctors' fees and equipment.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Track what is pending</h3><p>Insurer and corporate tie-up dues, amounts they disallow or deduct, and refunds owed to patients.</p></li>
      <li><h3>Review by doctor or service</h3><p>Which services and doctors actually cover their costs?</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="split-title">
  <div class="wrap two-col">
    <div>
      <h2 id="split-title">Why income types must be separate</h2>
      <p>Secondary sources explain that health care services by a clinical establishment are generally exempt from GST, but that does not make all of a clinic's receipts exempt.</p>
      <ul class="tick-list plain-list">
        <li>Sale of medicines or products may be treated differently</li>
        <li>Certain room charges have been reported as taxable above a limit, though sources differ on detail</li>
        <li>Cosmetic and elective work needs specific advice</li>
        <li>Non-medical income, such as renting space, may be taxable</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="para">Because purchases linked to exempt services may not give credit, a professional should look at your whole mix.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <h2>Payments that often need checking</h2>
      <dl class="fit-list">
        <div><dt>Visiting doctors</dt><dd>Are they employees or independent? Ask whether TDS applies, and keep agreements and monthly statements.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Staff</dt><dd>Nurses, receptionists and helpers need payroll records. PF and ESI may apply. See {% routeLink "/services/payroll-services/", "payroll" %}.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Rent and contractors</dt><dd>TDS may apply above limits. Ask your accountant to review.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Referral payments</dt><dd>Do not assume these are ordinary expenses. Medical ethics rules and tax rules may affect them, so ask a professional.</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="equip-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="equip-title" class="first-h2">Equipment and supplies</h2>
      <p>Expensive equipment is usually an asset, not a one-time expense. Keep a register listing the item, supplier, invoice, date, cost and any loan or lease. Maintenance contracts go under running costs. Consumables and medicines need stock tracking, with attention to expiries, or your costs will be guesses.</p>

      <h2 id="report">A monthly picture for the owner</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Income by service and by doctor</li>
        <li>Patient visits and average bill value</li>
        <li>Staff cost as a share of income</li>
        <li>Consumables as a share of procedure income</li>
        <li>Pending insurance or corporate dues</li>
        <li>Loan and lease repayments, against cash</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="owner">Whose accounts are these?</h2>
      <p>If you run the clinic alone as a proprietor, its results form part of your own return. If it is a partnership, LLP or company, it has its own compliance. Take advice before you change structure. For your own receipts from other hospitals, see {% routeLink "/industries/accounting-for-doctors/", "accounting for doctors" %}.</p>

      <h2 id="privacy">Keep patient data out of the accounts</h2>
      <p>Your accountant needs amounts, not diagnoses. Share billing summaries without clinical details, and agree who can see patient-level data.</p>

      <h2 id="ask">What to ask an accountant</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Have you worked with clinics or diagnostic centres?</li>
        <li>How will you separate exempt and taxable receipts?</li>
        <li>Can you report income by doctor and by service?</li>
        <li>How do you handle equipment loans and depreciation?</li>
      </ul>

